Chapter 2: Mathematical Foundations for Arm Modelling

2.1 Lie Groups and the Geometry of Rigid Motion

The configuration space of a rigid body is the special Euclidean group SE(3). An element  g = (R, p) \in SE(3)  consists of a rotation  R \in SO(3)  and a translation  p \in \mathbb{R}^3 . The group operation is  (R_1, p_1) \cdot (R_2, p_2) = (R_1 R_2, R_1 p_2 + p_1) . The Lie algebra  \mathfrak{se}(3)  consists of twists  \xi = (v, \omega)  with  v, \omega \in \mathbb{R}^3 . The exponential map  \exp: \mathfrak{se}(3) \to SE(3)  converts a twist into a rigid motion.

Relevant for the arm: The shoulder, elbow, and wrist joints are modelled as revolute joints whose screw axes are expressed in the base frame. The forward kinematics of a 7‑DoF arm is given by the product of exponentials (POE) formula:

T(\theta) = \exp([\mathcal{S}_1]\theta_1) \exp([\mathcal{S}_2]\theta_2) \cdots \exp([\mathcal{S}_7]\theta_7) M,

where  \mathcal{S}_i \in \mathbb{R}^6  are the screw axes (in base frame at home configuration),  \theta_i  are joint angles, and  M \in SE(3)  is the hand‑base home configuration.

Practical example – ABB YuMi: The YuMi collaborative robot arm has 7 DoF, and its redundancy is often parameterised using the SEW‑angle (swing–elevation–twist) representation [1]. This explicit parameterisation of the null‑space motion is a concrete realisation of the theoretical framework we develop.

2.2 Screw Theory and the Product of Exponentials

A screw axis for a revolute joint is defined by a unit direction vector  \omega  and a point  q  on the axis:  \mathcal{S} = [-\omega \times q; \ \omega] . For the arm, the shoulder screw axes are three concurrent revolute axes through the glenohumeral joint centre: flexion/extension (mediolateral axis), abduction/adduction (anterior‑posterior axis), and internal/external rotation (longitudinal humeral axis). The elbow flexion axis passes through the lateral epicondyle; the pronation/supination axis runs from the radial head to the distal radioulnar joint (a screw with small pitch). The wrist axes pass through the capitate bone.

The POE formulation is coordinate‑free, avoids Gimbal lock, and makes the group structure explicit – essential for analysing the redundant 7‑DoF arm where singularities occur when the elbow is locked or the wrist axes align.

2.3 Port‑Hamiltonian Dynamics for the Arm

The arm undergoes continuous motion punctuated by discrete events: hand‑free swing, contact establishment (touch), loaded manipulation, and contact release. A port‑Hamiltonian (PH) system on a manifold  \mathcal{X}  with Hamiltonian  H: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}  is:

\dot{x} = [J(x) - R(x)] \frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x) + g(x)u, \quad y = g(x)^T \frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x),

where  J(x)  is skew‑symmetric (interconnection),  R(x) \ge 0  (dissipation), and  (u, y)  are power‑conjugate ports.

For the arm with series‑elastic actuators (SEA) at shoulder and elbow, we augment the state with elastic deflections  \xi :

x = [q; p; \xi], \quad H(q,p,\xi) = \frac{1}{2} p^T M^{-1}(q) p + V(q) + \frac{1}{2} \xi^T K_s \xi,

where  q \in \mathbb{R}^7  are joint angles,  p  are momenta,  V(q)  includes gravitational potential (significant for the arm’s heavy segments),  K_s  are series‑elastic stiffnesses. This PH formulation preserves passivity, ensuring stable interaction with the environment – critical for collaborative robots.

Recent validation: Dona & Behera (2025) [8] show that PH‑based force‑impedance control achieves stable, physically interpretable interaction with fewer parameters than classical methods. Seo et al. (2025) [6] extend this to SE(3) geometric control, directly validating our choice of Lie group formulation.

2.4 Tendon‑Driven Actuation and Coupling Matrix

Many robot arms use tendon‑driven transmissions (e.g., in prosthetics or lightweight collaborative arms). Let  \ell \in \mathbb{R}^m  be tendon displacements,  f \in \mathbb{R}^m  tendon tensions, and  \theta \in \mathbb{R}^n  joint angles. The coupling matrix  P(\theta) \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}  satisfies  \Delta \ell = P(\theta) \Delta \theta  and, by virtual work,  \tau = P(\theta)^T f . If  P  is constant, the rank  r = \text{rank}(P) \le \min(m, n)  determines actuation. For underactuated arms ( m < n ), some joint directions are passively compliant.

Variable coupling: Recent work by Zhang (2024) [11] introduces task‑defined pulley design using eccentric pulleys of variable radii, yielding a nonlinearly coupled actuation where  P(q)  varies over the range of motion. This allows the arm to change its transmission ratio depending on configuration – a hardware solution to the Positioning–Compliance Paradox.

Chapter 3: Mathematical Foundations and Design Principles for the Robot Arm

Building upon the leg and hand frameworks, this chapter develops the complete geometric–design theory for the robot arm. The arm’s unique features – kinematic redundancy (seven degrees of freedom for a six‑dimensional task space), the positioning–compliance paradox, and the need for seamless integration with the hand – demand a rigorous mathematical treatment that also supports architectural design thinking. We present five interconnected proposals that together form the core of the arm framework.

---

3.1 Proposal 1: Corrected Two‑Sided Bounding Theorem for the Positioning–Compliance Paradox

The Positioning–Compliance Paradox states that high positioning accuracy (stiff arm) and high safety (compliant arm) are fundamentally at odds. In the leg framework, the Stability–Adaptability Paradox was captured by a two‑sided bound involving the condition number of the coupling matrix. The arm framework requires an analogous symmetric structure.

Definition 3.1 (Positioning Index and Task Compliance Index).
For an arm with n joint degrees of freedom and m independent actuators, let P \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m} be the coupling (actuation) matrix with rank r = \rank(P). Define

PI(P) = \frac{r}{n - r}, \qquad
TCI(P, t) = \frac{\|P P^{+} t\|}{\|t\|},

where t \in \mathbb{R}^n is a desired joint synergy (e.g., a typical reaching pattern) and P^{+} is the Moore‑Penrose pseudoinverse. The product PI \cdot TCI measures the effective positioning authority available for synergy t.

The following theorem establishes that this product is structurally fixed by the rank and the synergy alignment, while the condition number \kappa(P) = \sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min} governs the actuation effort required to realise it.

Theorem 3.1 (Two‑sided Positioning–Compliance bound).
Let P \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m} have rank r, condition number \kappa(P), and let t_1 = P P^{+} t be the orthogonal projection of the synergy t onto the actuated subspace \col(P). Then

\boxed{
\frac{1}{\kappa(P)} \cdot \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|}
\;\leq\;
PI(P) \cdot \frac{\|P^{+} t\|}{\|t\|}
\;\leq\;
\kappa(P) \cdot \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|}
}.

Furthermore, the product PI(P) \cdot TCI(P,t) equals \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|} exactly, independently of \kappa(P).

Proof.
Let the singular value decomposition of P be P = U \Sigma V^{\mathsf T} with

\Sigma = \begin{bmatrix} \Sigma_r & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix},\quad
\Sigma_r = \diag(\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_r),\ \sigma_1 \ge \cdots \ge \sigma_r > 0.

Partition U = [U_1 \ U_2] where U_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times r} spans \col(P). Write t = t_1 + t_2 with t_1 = U_1 U_1^{\mathsf T}t and t_2 \in \ker(P^{\mathsf T}). Then

TCI(P,t) = \frac{\|P P^{+} t\|}{\|t\|} = \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|},

PI(P) = \frac{r}{n-r}.

Hence PI(P) \cdot TCI(P,t) = \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|}, a quantity that depends only on the rank and the alignment of t with the actuated subspace – not on the singular values.

Now consider the normalised actuation effort E(P,t) = PI(P) \cdot \frac{\|P^{+} t\|}{\|t\|}. Using P^{+} = V \Sigma^{+} U^{\mathsf T},

\|P^{+} t\|^{2} = \|\Sigma_r^{-1} U_1^{\mathsf T} t\|^{2} = \sum_{i=1}^{r} \frac{(u_i^{\mathsf T} t)^{2}}{\sigma_i^{2}},

\|t_1\|^{2} = \sum_{i=1}^{r} (u_i^{\mathsf T} t)^{2}.

Because \sigma_r \le \sigma_i \le \sigma_1 for all i,

\frac{\|t_1\|}{\sigma_1} \le \|P^{+} t\| \le \frac{\|t_1\|}{\sigma_r}.

Multiplying by PI = r/(n-r) and dividing by \|t\| gives

\frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{TCI}{\sigma_1} \le E(P,t) \le \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{TCI}{\sigma_r}.

Since \sigma_1 = \kappa(P) \sigma_r, we obtain the desired two‑sided bound. ∎

Interpretation for arm design.
The theorem reveals that the fundamental trade‑off is not between PI and TCI – their product is fixed by the choice of r and the synergy direction. Rather, the condition number \kappa(P) determines how efficiently the arm can realise that product. An isotropic coupling (\kappa = 1) achieves the product with minimal effort. A highly anisotropic coupling (large \kappa) may require extremely large actuator forces to produce the same synergy, making the arm either unsafe (high impact forces) or energetically wasteful. Thus, the design goal is to choose P such that \kappa(P) is close to 1 for the synergies of interest, while r (and hence PI) is set by the required positioning accuracy.

