3.2 Proposal 2: Formal Treatment of Kinematic Redundancy

The arm’s seven degrees of freedom provide a one‑dimensional null space of the geometric Jacobian at generic configurations. This redundancy is a design resource, but it incurs an energetic cost. We formalise this trade‑off.

Definition 3.2 (Jacobian and null‑space projection).
Let J(\theta) \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times 7} be the geometric Jacobian mapping joint velocities \dot{\theta} to the end‑effector twist \dot{x}_{ee}. The null space \mathcal{N}(J) has dimension 7 - \rank(J). For non‑singular configurations, \rank(J) = 6, so \dim \mathcal{N}(J) = 1. The orthogonal projector onto the null space is

\Pi_{\mathcal{N}} = I - J^{+} J,

where J^{+} is the Moore‑Penrose pseudoinverse.

Definition 3.3 (Redundancy Exploitation Index, REI).
Given a desired end‑effector twist \dot{x}_{ee} and a secondary objective function \phi(\theta) (e.g., joint‑limit avoidance, obstacle clearance), choose the joint velocity

\dot{\theta} = J^{+} \dot{x}_{ee} + \alpha (I - J^{+} J) \nabla_{\theta} \phi(\theta),\qquad \alpha \ge 0.

The Redundancy Exploitation Index is

REI(\theta, \dot{x}_{ee}) = \frac{\| \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} \dot{\theta} \|}{\|\dot{\theta}\|} \in [0,1].

REI = 0 when \alpha = 0 (pure pseudoinverse solution); REI \to 1 when the null‑space component dominates the motion.

Theorem 3.2 (Energetic cost of redundancy exploitation).
The additional kinetic energy consumed by the null‑space motion is

\Delta T = \frac{1}{2} \dot{\theta}_{\text{null}}^{\mathsf T} M(\theta) \dot{\theta}_{\text{null}}
= \frac{\alpha^{2}}{2} \|\nabla\phi\|^{2}_{(I-J^{+}J) M (I-J^{+}J)},

where \| \cdot \|_{A} denotes the norm induced by matrix A. This quantity is non‑negative and strictly positive for any \alpha > 0 and \nabla\phi \notin \mathcal{N}(J)^{\perp}.

Proof.
Set \dot{\theta}_{\text{null}} = \alpha \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} \nabla\phi. Then

\Delta T = \frac{\alpha^{2}}{2} (\Pi_{\mathcal{N}} \nabla\phi)^{\mathsf T} M (\Pi_{\mathcal{N}} \nabla\phi)
= \frac{\alpha^{2}}{2} (\nabla\phi)^{\mathsf T} \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} M \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} \nabla\phi.

Since \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} is symmetric and idempotent, the quadratic form defines a norm unless the vector lies in the null space of \Pi_{\mathcal{N}} M \Pi_{\mathcal{N}}, which is exactly \mathcal{N}(J)^{\perp}. ∎

Design implication.
The parameter \alpha should be chosen to balance the benefit of the secondary objective (e.g., avoiding a collision) against the energy cost \Delta T. For prosthetic arms, minimising \Delta T prolongs battery life; for collaborative robots, it reduces heat generation. The framework allows explicit optimisation over \alpha given task priorities.

