3.3 Proposal 3: Rigorous Arm–Hand Port‑Hamiltonian Composition

A unified model of the upper limb requires connecting the arm (7 DoF) and the hand (variable DoF) at the wrist while preserving passivity – the property that the combined system cannot inject energy spontaneously. We achieve this through a Dirac structure at the wrist interface.

Definition 3.4 (Wrist Dirac structure).
Let the arm subsystem have a power port with effort e_{w}^{\text{arm}} (wrist velocity) and flow f_{w}^{\text{arm}} (wrist force). The hand subsystem has a complementary port with effort e_{w}^{\text{hand}} (negative of wrist force) and flow f_{w}^{\text{hand}} (wrist velocity). The Dirac structure \mathcal{D}_{w} enforces

\mathcal{D}_{w} = \left\{ (f_{w}^{\text{arm}}, e_{w}^{\text{arm}}, f_{w}^{\text{hand}}, e_{w}^{\text{hand}}) \;:\; 
f_{w}^{\text{arm}} + f_{w}^{\text{hand}} = 0,\; e_{w}^{\text{arm}} = e_{w}^{\text{hand}} \right\}.

Theorem 3.3 (Passivity of the composed upper limb).
Let the arm be a port‑Hamiltonian system with Hamiltonian H_{\text{arm}}, dissipation R_{\text{arm}} \ge 0, and external port at the shoulder; let the hand be a port‑Hamiltonian system with H_{\text{hand}}, R_{\text{hand}} \ge 0, and external ports at the fingertips. When connected through \mathcal{D}_{w}, the total Hamiltonian H_{\text{upper}} = H_{\text{arm}} + H_{\text{hand}} satisfies

\dot{H}_{\text{upper}} \;\le\; y_{\text{shoulder}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{shoulder}} + y_{\text{fingertip}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{fingertip}},

i.e., the composed system is passive with respect to the external ports.

Proof.
For each subsystem we have the power balance:

\dot{H}_{\text{arm}} = -(\nabla H_{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} R_{\text{arm}} \nabla H_{\text{arm}}
+ y_{\text{shoulder}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{shoulder}}
+ (e_{w}^{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{arm}},

\dot{H}_{\text{hand}} = -(\nabla H_{\text{hand}})^{\mathsf T} R_{\text{hand}} \nabla H_{\text{hand}}
+ y_{\text{fingertip}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{fingertip}}
+ (e_{w}^{\text{hand}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{hand}}.

Adding the two equations and using the Dirac structure constraints f_{w}^{\text{arm}} + f_{w}^{\text{hand}} = 0 and e_{w}^{\text{arm}} = e_{w}^{\text{hand}} yields

(e_{w}^{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{arm}} + (e_{w}^{\text{hand}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{hand}}
= (e_{w}^{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{arm}} - (e_{w}^{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} f_{w}^{\text{arm}} = 0.

Hence

\dot{H}_{\text{upper}} = -(\nabla H_{\text{arm}})^{\mathsf T} R_{\text{arm}} \nabla H_{\text{arm}}
- (\nabla H_{\text{hand}})^{\mathsf T} R_{\text{hand}} \nabla H_{\text{hand}}
+ y_{\text{shoulder}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{shoulder}}
+ y_{\text{fingertip}}^{\mathsf T} u_{\text{fingertip}}.

Since both dissipation terms are non‑positive, the inequality follows. ∎

Corollary 3.1. The combined arm–hand system cannot inject mechanical energy into the environment without active power input at the shoulder or fingertips. This property is essential for safe physical human‑robot interaction and for stable prosthetic control.

