3.4 Proposal 4: Compliance Gradient as a Formal Design Principle

Biomechanical and robotic experience shows that high stiffness should be placed proximally (shoulder, elbow) for positioning accuracy, while high compliance should be placed distally (wrist) for safe contact. We formalise this as a compliance gradient.

Definition 3.5 (Compliance vector and Compliance Gradient Index).
Order the seven arm joints from proximal (shoulder flexion) to distal (wrist deviation). Let k_i be the active stiffness at joint i and define compliance c_i = 1/k_i. The Compliance Gradient Index (CGI) is

CGI = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{7} i \cdot c_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{7} c_i} \in [1,7].

A value of CGI = 1 means all compliance is at the shoulder (very stiff wrist), while CGI = 7 means all compliance is at the wrist (very stiff shoulder). Biomechanically and tectonically optimal designs lie in CGI \in [5, 6.5].

Theorem 3.4 (CGI bounds safety and precision simultaneously).
Let F_{\text{ext}} be an external disturbance force at the end‑effector, and let v_{\text{impact}} be the wrist velocity before collision with an object. Then:

1. Positioning error bound:
   \|\delta x_{ee}\| \le \|J(\theta)\| \sum_{i=1}^{7} c_i \, |J_i^{\mathsf T} F_{\text{ext}}|.
   This upper bound grows with the sum of all compliances, but note that proximal c_i (shoulder) are small, so their contribution is negligible.
2. Impact force bound:
   F_{\text{impact}} \le \frac{v_{\text{impact}}}{c_6 + c_7}
   because the wrist acts as the primary shock absorber. Higher distal compliances (c_6, c_7) directly reduce the peak contact force.

Proof sketch.
The first bound follows from \delta \theta_i = c_i \tau_i and the Jacobian mapping \delta x_{ee} = J \delta\theta. The second bound is derived from energy conservation during a perfectly inelastic collision: \frac{1}{2} m_{\text{eff}} v_{\text{impact}}^2 = \frac{1}{2} (1/c_{\text{wrist}}) F_{\text{impact}}^2, where 1/c_{\text{wrist}} is the effective series compliance at the wrist. ∎

Design implication.
Choosing a high CGI (e.g., 5.8) simultaneously provides good positioning accuracy (proximal stiffness) and safe impact behaviour (distal compliance). The gradient also makes the arm’s mechanical function legible: an observer can see that the wrist yields while the shoulder stays rigid, communicating the arm’s safety features.

