3.6 Connecting Arm and Hand: A Unified Upper‑Limb Model

The arm framework does not stand alone; it is the first half of a complete upper‑limb theory. Table 3.2 maps the key concepts between the hand and arm frameworks, highlighting the mathematical duality and design inversion.

Table 3.2: Correspondence between hand and arm frameworks

Concept Hand Arm
Primary function Grasping (enclosure, manipulation) Positioning (endpoint placement)
Core paradox Coupling–Adaptation Positioning–Compliance
Key index AI = (n-r)/r PI = r/(n-r)
Passive subspace role Used for shape adaptation Provides impact compliance
Redundancy type Actuation redundancy (underactuation) Kinematic redundancy (null space)
Tectonic proxy Tendon Visibility Index Actuator Visibility Index
Legibility metric Grip Intent Legibility Reach Intent Legibility
Pattern language scales Finger, hand, task Joint, limb, task

The two frameworks are composable via the wrist Dirac structure (Section 3.3). In a complete design, the arm’s compliance gradient (high proximal stiffness, low distal stiffness) flows naturally into the hand’s high adaptation index (compliant fingers). This produces a unified mechanical logic: rigid near the body for accurate positioning, progressively more compliant toward the fingertips for safe and adaptive interaction.

