Summary of Mathematical Contributions

This chapter has provided:

1. A corrected two‑sided bounding theorem (Theorem 3.1) that separates the structurally fixed product PI\cdot TCI from the effort scaling controlled by \kappa(P).
2. A formal treatment of kinematic redundancy (Theorem 3.2) quantifying the energetic cost of exploiting the null space, with the Redundancy Exploitation Index REI.
3. A rigorous composition of arm and hand via Dirac structures (Theorem 3.3) proving passivity of the combined upper limb – a critical safety guarantee.
4. The Compliance Gradient Index CGI (Theorem 3.4) linking distal compliance to impact safety and proximal stiffness to positioning accuracy.
5. A complete technical specification for a reference 7‑DoF arm, including POE parameters, inertial data, series‑elastic stiffness values, and the effort index EI.

These mathematical foundations support the spatial grammar, pattern language, and tectonic principles developed in the following chapters, and they enable a seamless integration with the existing hand framework. The next chapter will apply these tools to generate arm movements and design patterns for collaborative and prosthetic applications.

Chapter 4: Architectural‑Design Framework for the Robot Arm

Building upon the mathematical foundations of Chapter 3, this chapter develops a design‑oriented language for the robot arm. The arm is not only a kinematic chain but also a physical artifact that must be understood, trusted, and operated by humans – whether in collaborative industrial settings, prosthetics, or rehabilitation. Architectural thinking – spatial grammars, pattern languages, tectonics – provides the necessary vocabulary to bridge mathematical precision and human‑centred design.

We organise the chapter as follows. Section 4.1 introduces a spatial grammar for generating arm movements and reaching sequences. Section 4.2 presents a pattern language at three scales (joint, limb, task) with full Alexandrian documentation. Section 4.3 reframes tectonics as mechanical legibility for the arm, defining quantitative proxies (Actuator Visibility Index, Joint Articulation Exposure Score, Reach Intent Legibility Score) and linking them to safety and collaboration. Section 4.4 discusses adaptability as a layered design principle, mapping Brand’s architectural layers to arm subsystems.

---

4.1 Spatial Grammar for Arm Movements

A spatial grammar is a rule‑based system for generating valid arm configurations and movement sequences. It provides a formal link between symbolic design intentions (e.g., “reach to a cup”) and the continuous joint‑angle trajectories analysed in Chapter 3.

4.1.1 Algebraic Definition

Define the arm grammar \mathcal{G}_{\text{arm}} = (\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{C}) where:

· Primitives \mathcal{P} are fundamental movement elements, each represented as a one‑parameter subgroup of SE(3) or as an explicit joint displacement. For the 7‑DoF arm:
  \begin{aligned}
  &\text{Elevate}(\phi) : \theta_1 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(shoulder flexion)} \\
  &\text{Abduct}(\phi) : \theta_2 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(shoulder abduction)} \\
  &\text{RotShoulder}(\phi) : \theta_3 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(humeral rotation)} \\
  &\text{FlexElbow}(\phi) : \theta_4 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(elbow flexion)} \\
  &\text{Pronate}(\phi) : \theta_5 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(forearm pronation)} \\
  &\text{FlexWrist}(\phi) : \theta_6 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(wrist flexion)} \\
  &\text{DeviateWrist}(\phi) : \theta_7 \leftarrow \phi \quad &\text{(wrist deviation)} \\
  &\text{Reach}(T_{\text{target}}) : \text{composite} &\text{(see below)} \\
  &\text{Contact}(S, p) : \text{event} &\text{(hand touches surface S at point p)} \\
  &\text{Release} : \text{event} &\text{(hand leaves contact)}
  \end{aligned}
  Each primitive has a continuous parameter \phi within joint limits (see Table 3.1). The primitive \text{Reach}(T_{\text{target}}) is a macro that expands into a sequence of joint motions computed via inverse kinematics, optionally using the null space for secondary objectives (see Theorem 3.2).
· Rules \mathcal{R} are composition operators:
  · \text{Seq}(\alpha, \beta) : execute \alpha then \beta.
  · \text{Par}(\alpha, \beta) : execute \alpha and \beta simultaneously (e.g., shoulder elevation and elbow flexion together).
  · \text{Repeat}(\alpha, n) : execute \alpha n times consecutively.
  · \text{Cond}(\text{cond}, \alpha, \beta) : execute \alpha if condition holds, else \beta (e.g., if contact detected, stop).
  · \text{Transport}(\alpha, \text{load}) : execute \alpha while maintaining a load (activates gravity compensation).
  · \text{Bilateral}(\alpha, \delta) : coordinate both arms with phase offset \delta (for dual‑arm robots or bilateral prosthetics).
· Constraints \mathcal{C} are inequalities or equalities that must be satisfied by any valid derivation:
  · Joint limits: \theta_i^{\min} \le \theta_i \le \theta_i^{\max}.
  · Self‑collision avoidance: \text{dist}(\text{segment}_i, \text{segment}_j) \ge \epsilon.
  · Singularity avoidance: \det(J(\theta)J(\theta)^T) \ge \delta.
  · Impingement avoidance: for shoulder elevation, the subacromial space must remain non‑zero; we encode this as |\theta_1| \le 120^\circ or a more complex inequality.
  · Energy budget: \int_0^T \tau^T \dot{\theta} dt \le E_{\max}.
  · Force closure during contact: \rank(G) = 6 where G is the grasp matrix (hand framework).

4.1.2 Example Derivation: Reaching to Grasp a Cup

Starting from a home configuration \theta = 0 (arm at side, wrist neutral), the grammar generates a reaching motion:

```
1. Par(Elevate(30°), FlexElbow(40°))   // gross positioning
2. Seq(
       RotShoulder(15°),                // bring hand forward
       Pronate(20°)                     // orient palm toward cup
   )
3. Reach(T_cup)                         // fine adjustment using inverse kinematics
4. Contact(cup_surface, grasp_point)   // trigger impact reset map
5. Transport(Retract, cup_mass)        // lift with gravity compensation
```

The Reach primitive expands to an optimisation problem: find \theta such that T_{\text{arm}}(\theta) = T_{\text{target}} while minimising a secondary cost (e.g., deviation from neutral posture). The null‑space projection (Theorem 3.2) provides the solution.

4.1.3 Expressiveness and Relation to Configuration Space

The grammar is parametrised because each primitive carries a continuous parameter \phi. For any configuration \theta within joint limits, we can construct a derivation: simply apply a sequence of \text{Elevate}, \text{Abduct}, \dots with the exact joint angles. Therefore, the set of reachable configurations is the entire configuration space \mathcal{C} = [\theta_1^{\min},\theta_1^{\max}] \times \cdots \times [\theta_7^{\min},\theta_7^{\max}], which is a 7‑dimensional box. Conversely, a given derivation maps to a unique \theta (or a small set if redundancy is resolved). The grammar thus provides a high‑level symbolic language for arm motion, while the mathematics of Chapter 3 ensures consistency with kinematics and dynamics.

