4.4 Adaptability as a Layered Design Philosophy

Brand’s (1994) six layers of building adaptation – site, structure, skin, services, space plan, stuff – map naturally to arm design. Each layer changes at a different timescale, and designing for adaptability means considering all layers.

Table 4.1: Layered adaptability for the robot arm

Brand’s layer Arm analogue Timescale Design consideration
Site Base mounting (shoulder attachment) Never changes Standardised interface (e.g., ISO 9409)
Structure Link lengths, joint types Decades Modular links, replaceable segments
Skin Exterior shell, covers Years Removable panels, customisable colours
Services Actuators, tendons, electronics Months to years Plug‑and‑play motor modules, quick‑disconnect cables
Space plan Control software, motion planning Days to weeks API for reprogramming, user‑defined primitives
Stuff Real‑time adaptation (e.g., impedance tuning) Milliseconds to seconds Online stiffness/position adjustment

Polyvalence (Hertzberger 2005) – designing elements that accommodate multiple uses without physical change – is realised in the arm by the compliance gradient (L3) and the null‑space (L4). A well‑designed arm does not need to be reconfigured to switch from precision assembly (high PI) to safe collaboration (high AI); the compliance gradient automatically provides wrist compliance while maintaining shoulder stiffness.

Design for multiple timescales: The port‑Hamiltonian framework (Chapter 3) naturally supports layered adaptability because the interconnection structure J(x) and dissipation R(x) can be modified online (services layer) without altering the underlying mechanical structure. This is a key advantage over classical controllers that treat the arm as a fixed plant.

