5.2 Wrist Dirac Structure: Rigorous Composition of Arm and Hand

We already introduced the wrist Dirac structure in Chapter 3.3 (Definition 3.4 and Theorem 3.3). Here we extend that composition to include the tendon‑driven wrist as an active element.

Definition 5.1 (Wrist with variable transmission). Let the wrist joint have its own coupling matrix P_{\text{wrist}} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times m_{\text{wrist}}} (two degrees of freedom: flexion and deviation). The wrist’s port‑Hamiltonian dynamics are:

\dot{x}_{\text{wrist}} = [J_{\text{wrist}} - R_{\text{wrist}}] \nabla H_{\text{wrist}} + g_{\text{wrist}} u_{\text{wrist}}, \quad y_{\text{wrist}} = g_{\text{wrist}}^T \nabla H_{\text{wrist}},

with state x_{\text{wrist}} = (q_{\text{wrist}}, p_{\text{wrist}}, \xi_{\text{wrist}}) where \xi_{\text{wrist}} are tendon deflections. The wrist’s output port (forces and velocities at the hand attachment) is connected to the hand’s input port via the Dirac structure \mathcal{D}_w defined in Definition 3.4.

Theorem 5.1 (Passivity of the combined arm–wrist–hand system). Let the arm be a PH system with Hamiltonian H_{\text{arm}}, the wrist be a PH system with H_{\text{wrist}}, and the hand be a PH system with H_{\text{hand}}. When connected through \mathcal{D}_w at the wrist and \mathcal{D}_h at the hand–object interface, the total Hamiltonian H_{\text{total}} = H_{\text{arm}} + H_{\text{wrist}} + H_{\text{hand}} satisfies:

\dot{H}_{\text{total}} \le y_{\text{shoulder}}^T u_{\text{shoulder}} + y_{\text{object}}^T u_{\text{object}}.

The proof follows the same steps as Theorem 3.3, adding the wrist subsystem and using the fact that Dirac structures are power‑continuous at each interface.

Corollary 5.1. The upper limb’s endpoint impedance at the fingertip is the composition of the arm’s impedance, the wrist’s impedance, and the hand’s impedance. For a well‑designed arm with compliance gradient (L3) and hand with high AI, the endpoint impedance is naturally stable and safe.

