5.3 Unified Design Methodology

We now synthesise the mathematical, architectural, and compositional tools into a step‑by‑step methodology for designing an upper limb (arm + hand) for a given application (prosthetic, collaborative robot, rehabilitation exoskeleton).

Step 1 – Task analysis and pattern selection

· List the required tasks (e.g., pick‑and‑place, tool use, power lifting, precision assembly).
· For each task, select the appropriate arm task‑scale patterns (T1–T5) and hand grasp patterns (e.g., H1 Power Grasp, H2 Precision Pinch).
· Use the grammar to generate candidate motion sequences.

Step 2 – Configuration space and actuation design

· Choose number of DoF for arm (typically 7) and hand (e.g., 15 underactuated).
· Decide actuation rank r_{\text{arm}} and r_{\text{hand}} based on required PI and AI. For a collaborative arm, r_{\text{arm}} \approx 5 (actuated shoulder+elbow, passive wrist). For a prosthetic hand, r_{\text{hand}} \approx 1-2 (high AI).
· Design the coupling matrix P (or P(q) for variable transmission) such that \kappa(P) is near 1 for the expected synergies.

Step 3 – Compliance gradient and series‑elastic placement

· Use the CGI formula (Definition 3.5) to assign stiffness values to the seven arm joints, ensuring monotonic increase in compliance distally (CGI ∈ [5,6.5]).
· For the hand, ensure that the overall compliance from wrist to fingertip continues the gradient (wrist stiffer than fingers, fingers highly compliant).
· Place series‑elastic elements at joints where impact absorption is critical (wrist, possibly elbow).

Step 4 – Tectonic design for legibility

· Compute target AVI, JAES, RILS based on application requirements (e.g., high legibility for collaborative robots, moderate for prosthetics where discretion may be preferred).
· Implement design guidelines from Section 4.3.4: transparent covers, visible cables, articulated joint markings, colour‑coding of axes.

Step 5 – Control architecture

· Implement the port‑Hamiltonian controller with energy tanks to ensure passivity (see Seo et al. 2025 [6]).
· For the null space, choose the secondary objective \phi and gain \alpha to balance energy cost (Theorem 3.2) and obstacle avoidance.
· For the wrist Dirac structure, ensure that the hand’s force commands are transmitted to the arm without violating passivity.

Step 6 – Optimisation and iteration

· Use the theoretical design space exploration (Section 5.4) to adjust parameters (link lengths, stiffness values, coupling ratios) to meet performance targets.
· Simulate using the port‑Hamiltonian simulator (Appendix D of the hand dissertation, adapted to 7 DoF).
· If legibility scores are too low, iterate on tectonic design.

Example application – Prosthetic arm + myoelectric hand:

· Arm: 7 DoF, but only 4 active motors (r=4, PI=4/3). Shoulder and elbow are actively controlled; wrist and forearm pronation are passive with springs (CGI≈5.5).
· Hand: underactuated (AI high) with two motors (one for thumb opposition, one for finger coupling).
· Tectonics: transparent socket, visible cables along the forearm, colour‑coded joints.
· The unified methodology ensures that the high AI hand and moderate PI arm together produce a functional, legible, and safe prosthesis.

