5.4 Theoretical Pareto Analysis of Upper‑Limb Trade‑offs

We explore the design space of a 7‑DoF arm (actuation rank r) combined with an underactuated hand (adaptation index AI_{\text{hand}}). Two conflicting objectives:

1. Positioning accuracy – approximated by PI_{\text{arm}} = r/(7-r) (higher is better).
2. Safety / adaptability – approximated by 1/F_{\text{impact}} (from Theorem 3.4) and AI_{\text{hand}}.

We also include tectonic legibility (RILS) as a third objective, though it is often correlated with safety.

Table 5.2: Pareto‑optimal configurations for a collaborative upper limb

Configuration r_{\text{arm}} PI_{\text{arm}} AI_{\text{hand}} CGI RILS (est.) Application
A (stiff arm, stiff hand) 7 ∞ 0 1.2 0.5 Industrial pick‑and‑place (no human contact)
B (moderate arm, high adaptation hand) 5 2.5 2.0 5.8 0.8 Collaborative robot, prosthetic (balanced)
C (underactuated arm, very high adaptation hand) 3 0.75 4.0 6.5 0.9 Soft collaborative, rehabilitation (safety first)
D (fully actuated arm, high adaptation hand) 7 ∞ 2.0 6.0 0.7 High‑precision surgery with soft hand (e.g., RBO Hand)

Observation: Configuration B (r=5, AI_hand≈2, CGI≈5.8) dominates the Pareto frontier for most collaborative and prosthetic applications. It provides sufficient positioning authority while maintaining safety through wrist compliance and hand adaptation. The high CGI ensures that the compliance gradient is monotonic, and RILS is high because the arm’s joints are visible and the wrist clearly yields.

Theorem 5.2 (Upper‑limb performance bound). For a given total actuator mass M_{\text{act}} and total number of degrees of freedom n_{\text{total}} = n_{\text{arm}} + n_{\text{hand}}, the sum PI_{\text{arm}} + AI_{\text{hand}} is bounded above by a function of M_{\text{act}} and the transmission efficiency. Specifically,

PI_{\text{arm}} + AI_{\text{hand}} \le \frac{M_{\text{act}}}{m_{\text{min}}} \cdot \eta_{\text{max}},

where m_{\text{min}} is the minimum actuator mass per DoF and \eta_{\text{max}} the maximum transmission efficiency.

Proof sketch. Each actuator can contribute to either increasing r_{\text{arm}} (increasing PI) or increasing the effective degrees of freedom in the hand (decreasing r_{\text{hand}}, increasing AI). The total actuation mass is the sum over actuators. By reallocating actuators from arm to hand (or vice versa), one trades PI for AI. The inequality follows from the physical limits of actuator power density and transmission losses. ∎

This theorem quantifies the fundamental resource trade‑off: a designer cannot simultaneously achieve high PI (stiff, accurate arm) and high AI (compliant, adaptive hand) without a proportional increase in actuation mass or efficiency. It justifies the use of underactuation (variable transmission) and series elasticity to “cheat” the bound by effectively increasing \eta without adding mass.

