6.1 Summary of Contributions

This dissertation has developed a unified geometric–design framework for the robot arm that integrates advanced mathematical modelling with architectural thinking. The work is positioned as the third pillar of a comprehensive theory of the upper limb, complementing prior frameworks for the leg and the hand. The specific contributions are as follows.

6.1.1 Mathematical Contributions

Two‑sided bounding theorem for the Positioning–Compliance Paradox (Theorem 3.1).
We proved that the product PI(P) \cdot TCI(P,t) = \frac{r}{n-r} \cdot \frac{\|t_1\|}{\|t\|} is structurally fixed by the rank and synergy alignment, while the actuation effort required to realise it is bounded by the condition number \kappa(P). This corrects earlier one‑sided formulations and establishes a symmetric relationship linking design parameters (rank, condition number) to task‑level performance. The theorem demonstrates that high positioning authority (large PI) inevitably reduces the subspace for passive compliance, and that isotropic coupling (\kappa \approx 1) is essential for efficient realisation of desired synergies.

Recently, Califano and colleagues have independently advanced a geometric task‑space port‑Hamiltonian formulation for redundant manipulators that similarly splits the Hamiltonian momentum variable into task‑space and null‑space components. This convergence validates the theoretical approach taken in this dissertation and indicates that the geometric mechanics community is moving toward the same foundational structures we have adopted.

Formal treatment of kinematic redundancy (Theorem 3.2).
We introduced the Redundancy Exploitation Index REI \in [0,1] to quantify the fraction of joint velocity devoted to secondary objectives, and proved that null‑space motion incurs a non‑negative kinetic energy penalty \Delta T = \frac{\alpha^2}{2}\|\nabla\phi\|^{2}_{(I-J^{+}J)M(I-J^{+}J)}. This provides a rigorous basis for trading off energy efficiency against obstacle avoidance, joint‑limit satisfaction, and posture optimisation.

Recent work on unified arm angle solutions for SRS robots has established complete frameworks for determining feasible arm angle intervals and selecting global configurations to avoid joint singularities. These advances directly support the practical application of our REI framework, providing the analytical inverse kinematics necessary to realise null‑space control.

Passive composition of arm and hand via Dirac structures (Theorem 3.3).
We proved that the combined upper limb system preserves passivity when arm and hand are connected through the wrist Dirac structure, guaranteeing that the system cannot inject energy spontaneously – a critical safety property for prosthetic and collaborative applications. A recent force‑impedance controller design in the port‑Hamiltonian framework has demonstrated that fewer control parameters are needed to achieve asymptotic stability compared to Euler–Lagrange approaches, and that a clear physical interpretation follows from the PH formalism. This validates our choice of PH methodology for upper‑limb composition.

Compliance Gradient Index (Theorem 3.4).
We introduced CGI = \frac{\sum i\cdot c_i}{\sum c_i} \in [1,7] to formalise the biomechanical principle that compliance should increase monotonically from proximal to distal joints. The theorem bounds endpoint positioning error and impact force in terms of CGI, providing a quantitative tool for arm design. Recent developments in variable stiffness actuators, including tensegrity‑based designs that eliminate direct contact between rigid elements to reduce internal mechanical friction, and worm gear‑based designs that decouple driving motors from external forces, provide hardware platforms that can realise the compliance gradient principle in practice.

6.1.2 Design Contributions

Spatial grammar for arm movements (Section 4.1).
We defined a parametrised grammar \mathcal{G}_{\text{arm}} = (\mathcal{P}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{C}) with primitives (Elevate, Abduct, Reach, Contact, Transport), rules (Seq, Par, Repeat, Cond, Transport, Bilateral), and constraints (joint limits, self‑collision avoidance, singularity avoidance, energy budget). The grammar provides a direct mapping from symbolic design intentions to continuous joint‑angle trajectories, bridging the gap between human‑centric task descriptions and mathematical models.

Pattern language for arm design (Section 4.2).
We documented 15 patterns across three scales (joint, limb, task), each with full Alexandrian documentation including context, problem, solution, mathematical realisation, force closure conditions, restraining forces, variations, and related patterns. The pattern language captures recurring design knowledge in a form that is accessible to designers while remaining mathematically precise.

Tectonic proxies for mechanical legibility (Section 4.3).
We introduced three quantitative proxies: Actuator Visibility Index (AVI), Joint Articulation Exposure Score (JAES), and Reach Intent Legibility Score (RILS). These link mechanical expression to user acceptance and safety. Theorem 4.1 established that RILS is bounded above by AVI and JAES, formalising the intuition that visible mechanics enable legible motion.

Recent empirical research has demonstrated that opaque robot policies lacking transparent intent explanations cause a 36% drop in task success, a 17‑fold increase in safety incidents, and a 44% increase in task completion time compared to transparent baselines. Studies of humanoid robot arm movements have shown that combining gaze with pointing makes robot actions clearer (multimodal superiority), while gaze alone offers the fastest clue (oculomotor primacy). Furthermore, a comprehensive experiment with 56 participants found that robot arm speed had a greater impact on self‑reported measures such as surprise and fear, while arm size and degrees of freedom had a greater influence on task performance and human behaviour. These findings directly validate the importance of our tectonic approach: mechanical legibility (AVI, JAES) and motion legibility (RILS) are not aesthetic niceties but functional requirements for safe and efficient human‑robot collaboration.

6.1.3 Integration Contributions

Formal mapping between arm and hand frameworks (Table 5.1).
We established a complete conceptual correspondence, showing that the arm’s Positioning Index is the reciprocal of the hand’s Adaptation Index, reflecting the fundamental design inversion: the arm prioritises active control (high PI), while the hand prioritises passive adaptation (high AI). The same mathematical objects appear in both frameworks, but their interpretation and design priority are inverted – a theoretical unification that has not previously been articulated.

Unified design methodology (Section 5.3).
We proposed a six‑step methodology for designing upper limbs: task analysis and pattern selection, configuration space and actuation design, compliance gradient and series‑elastic placement, tectonic design for legibility, control architecture, and optimisation and iteration. This methodology synthesises mathematical rigour with human‑centred design considerations.

Theoretical Pareto analysis and performance bound (Theorem 5.2).
We proved that PI_{\text{arm}} + AI_{\text{hand}} \le \frac{M_{\text{act}}}{m_{\text{min}}} \cdot \eta_{\text{max}}, quantifying the fundamental resource trade‑off between positioning authority and adaptive capability. This bound justifies the use of underactuation and series elasticity to effectively “cheat” the bound by increasing transmission efficiency without adding mass.

Recent advances in co‑design have demonstrated that simultaneous optimisation of hardware and control policies can achieve superior performance. A concurrent design approach for mobile manipulators that optimises key arm‑mounting parameters directly for task performance using Bayesian optimisation has produced novel designs that outperform heuristic‑based performance indices. An application‑driven co‑design framework for 6‑DOF manipulators has shown that higher performance for specific applications can be achieved by simultaneously optimising motion and motors across multiple tasks. A cross‑embodied co‑design framework for dexterous hands has demonstrated that task‑specific hand morphology and complementary control policies can be learned together, enabling design, training, fabrication, and deployment in under 24 hours. These developments validate the co‑design philosophy embedded in our unified methodology and suggest concrete computational approaches for implementing it.

