6.2 Theoretical Implications

6.2.1 Geometry as a Unifying Language

The use of SE(3) Lie groups and screw theory throughout this work demonstrates that geometry provides a natural bridge between the continuous world of physics and the discrete world of design patterns. The same mathematical structures – product of exponentials, twist coordinates, Jacobian mappings – appear in the kinematics of the arm, the hand, and the leg, suggesting that a truly unified theory of the human body or its robotic analogues is possible within the geometric mechanics framework.

Recent work on kinematic parameter identification using SE(3) Lie group error modelling has shown that screw‑error formulations avoid the non‑uniqueness and singularity issues of Euler angles under large rotations, ensuring geometric consistency and global differentiability. The cohomological equation for screw motions on SE(3) has been studied, providing a precise description of obstruction phenomena relevant to robotic rigid‑body motion. These developments further validate the geometric approach taken in this dissertation.

6.2.2 Layered Adaptability as a Unifying Framework

The mapping of Brand’s architectural layers to arm subsystems (Table 4.1) reveals that adaptability must be considered at multiple timescales simultaneously. The port‑Hamiltonian formulation naturally supports this layered view because the interconnection structure J(x) and dissipation R(x) can be modified online (services layer) without altering the underlying mechanical structure. This is a key insight for designing arms that can adapt to changing tasks and environments without requiring complete redesign.

6.2.3 Polyvalence and Underactuation

Hertzberger’s concept of polyvalence – designing elements that accommodate multiple uses without physical change – is realised in underactuated mechanisms. The Adaptation Index AI quantifies polyvalence in mechanical terms: higher AI means the arm or hand can adapt to more object shapes without reconfiguration. The trade‑off between polyvalence (high AI) and positioning accuracy (high PI) is captured by Theorem 3.1, providing a theoretical foundation for balancing these competing objectives.

Recent research on underactuated mechanisms has produced ultra‑light robotic prosthetic hands that enable both precision fingertip control and shape‑adaptive gripping through simple motion commands, using a linkage‑wire hybrid actuation system. This work validates the practical value of underactuation as a polyvalence mechanism. A compact variable stiffness wrist using parallel architecture and only four motors has been developed, demonstrating the ability to achieve high accuracy and disturbance rejection in rigid configurations while minimising interaction forces with compliant behaviour. These hardware innovations provide concrete embodiments of the theoretical principles developed in this dissertation.

6.2.4 Tectonics and Mechanical Legibility

The tectonic proxies developed in this work link mechanical design to cognitive outcomes. The demonstration that opaque policies degrade task success and increase safety incidents, and that multimodal cues improve legibility in human‑robot interaction, provides empirical grounding for the claim that mechanical legibility is not merely aesthetic but functional. The framework thus contributes to the growing field of human‑robot interaction by providing quantitative tools for designing mechanically legible systems.

---

6.3 Limitations

6.3.1 Theoretical Limitations

Constant coupling matrix assumption. The bounding theorem (Theorem 3.1) assumes a constant coupling matrix P. In practice, tendon‑driven mechanisms often exhibit configuration‑dependent coupling due to tendon wrapping and variable moment arms. While we have discussed variable transmission (Section 3.4.6), the full analysis of time‑varying or state‑dependent P(q) is not developed. Recent research on reconfigurable tendon‑driven robots with lockable joints has shown that inter‑segment coupling can be eliminated by selectively actuating targeted segments, fundamentally avoiding the requirement for complex coordinated control. This suggests that a hybrid approach – constant coupling per mode, with discrete mode switching – may be more tractable than full continuous variability.

Infinite PI for fully actuated arms. The definition PI = r/(n-r) yields infinite PI for fully actuated arms (r=n). While mathematically consistent, this obscures the distinction between “very high” and “infinite” positioning authority. In practice, sensor noise, actuator saturation, and joint flexibility impose practical limits. A normalised index, such as PI_{\text{norm}} = (2r - n)/n \in [-1, 1], may be more suitable for design optimisation. We have retained the original definition for theoretical consistency with the hand framework, but practitioners should be aware of this limitation.

Lack of empirical validation for tectonic proxies. The proxies AVI, JAES, and RILS are theoretical constructs. While we have cited related empirical studies on legibility and transparency, the specific relationships between these proxies and user outcomes have not been experimentally validated. The theorem bounding RILS by AVI and JAES (Theorem 4.1) relies on assumptions about observer strategy that have not been tested.

6.3.2 Scope Limitations

Fixed topology of the arm model. We assume the arm has exactly seven revolute joints in a serial chain. This is appropriate for anthropomorphic arms but excludes parallel mechanisms, continuum arms, or arms with additional prismatic joints. Recent research on continuum soft robotic arms has challenged the assumption that distributed compliance and high‑performance control are incompatible, demonstrating accurate and repeatable execution of dynamic Cartesian tasks on a fully continuum arm. Extending our framework to continuum arms would require generalising screw theory to infinite‑dimensional systems, a non‑trivial extension.

Simplified impact dynamics. The impact reset map (Section 3.3) assumes perfectly inelastic collisions without considering surface compliance, friction anisotropy, or multiple simultaneous contacts. For prosthetic and collaborative applications, these effects can be significant. The null space compliance approach for maintaining safety and tracking performance in human‑robot interactions has introduced modified Cartesian impedance control methods combined with dynamic system‑based motion generators to enhance interaction capability without compromising task tracking, proving passivity of the system on a 7‑DOF KUKA LWR IV+ robot. This points toward more sophisticated impact models that could be integrated.

