6.4 Future Research Directions

6.4.1 Empirical Validation of Tectonic Proxies

Priority research direction. Conduct user studies with physical arm prototypes or high‑fidelity VR simulations to measure how AVI, JAES, and RILS correlate with task performance, trust, safety, and abandonment rates. Use ISO/TS 15066 collision force limits as ground truth for safety. The experimental paradigm should follow the three‑stage structure established in prior frameworks: expert survey correlating AVI and JAES with perceived functional transparency, naïve‑observer study measuring RILS as prediction accuracy of next arm movement from static posture photographs (or early dynamic trajectories), and amputee/operator study measuring satisfaction and cognitive load.

Recent research on biomimetic trust‑based robot‑to‑human object handover has shown that human trust significantly impacts biomechanics: when trust in the robot decreased, human hand movement slowed down, the angle between human hand and robot arm increased, and human grip forces increased. The same study demonstrated that a novel handover mechanism using kinematic redundancy to reduce potential impulse forces significantly improved transparency, naturalness, engagement, cooperation, cognitive workload, and human trust. These findings provide a methodological template for validating our tectonic proxies: measured changes in user behaviour (movement speed, posture, grip force) can serve as objective correlates of perceived legibility.

6.4.2 Extension to Variable Stiffness Actuators

Research direction. Incorporate variable stiffness actuators (VSAs) into the port‑Hamiltonian framework by augmenting the state with stiffness parameters k and adding control ports for stiffness modulation. The coupling matrix P would become a function of both configuration \theta and stiffness k: P = P(\theta, k). The Hamiltonian would include the elastic energy stored in variable springs, and the dissipation matrix could be modulated with stiffness to achieve optimal energy efficiency.

Recent work on variable stiffness tensegrity‑based compliant actuators has demonstrated low damping and compliant behaviour, a wide range of stiffness adjustments, and decoupling of rotational stiffness from rotation angle by utilising MACCEPA variants. The WAVE actuator achieves decoupling of external forces from actuators through a non‑backdrivable worm gear, allowing robots to switch continuously between high stiffness (for precise actuation) and low stiffness (for safe interaction). These hardware platforms provide concrete systems for implementing VSA‑based arm control.

6.4.3 Learning of Grammars and Patterns

Research direction. Use grammar induction algorithms to learn arm movement patterns from human demonstration data. The spatial grammar \mathcal{G}_{\text{arm}} defined in Section 4.1 could serve as a prior for Bayesian inverse reinforcement learning, enabling personalised prosthetic control that adapts to individual user movement styles. The pattern language could be extended to include probabilistic weights, allowing the system to select the most likely pattern given observed task context.

Recent advances in Lagrangian neural network‑based control have demonstrated the ability to learn unknown system dynamics while preserving physical structure. Hamiltonian neural networks for system identification and control of robotic systems have shown that the port‑Hamiltonian framework guarantees passivity by construction. These methods could be adapted to learn the coupling matrix P and compliance parameters from data, enabling automatic identification of the arm’s mathematical model from observed behaviour.

6.4.4 Full Upper‑Limb Co‑design

Research direction. Develop an integrated optimisation framework that simultaneously optimises arm and hand parameters (link lengths, joint placements, stiffness values, coupling ratios, actuator selections) for a given task distribution. The theoretical bounds from Theorem 3.1 and Theorem 5.2 can serve as constraints or regularisers, while the compliance gradient (CGI) and tectonic proxies (AVI, JAES, RILS) can be incorporated as objectives.

The developments in co‑design mentioned earlier – task‑driven co‑design of mobile manipulators, application‑oriented co‑design of motors and motions, and cross‑embodied co‑design for dexterous hands – provide methodological templates. These approaches typically use Bayesian optimisation or differentiable simulation to explore the design space and can be adapted to the arm–hand system. The goal would be an end‑to‑end pipeline that can design, fabricate, and deploy an optimised upper limb for a specific user and task set.

6.4.5 Two‑Arm Coordination

Research direction. Extend the spatial grammar to support dual‑arm coordination with inter‑arm constraints (e.g., avoiding self‑collision, load sharing, synchronised grasping). The rule \text{Bilateral}(\alpha, \delta) defined in Section 4.1 is a placeholder; a full two‑arm grammar would require the composition of two arm systems through a shared object or task frame. The port‑Hamiltonian composition can be extended via Dirac structures at the mutual contact points between the arms and the manipulated object.

Recent research on compliant force control for robots has surveyed strategies for enabling safe and effective interaction with dynamic and uncertain environments, highlighting the importance of passivity and energy‑based methods. A null space compliance approach for maintaining safety and tracking performance in HRI has been validated on a 7‑DOF KUKA LWR IV+ robot. These foundations can be extended to dual‑arm systems by formulating the coordination problem as a multi‑agent port‑Hamiltonian system with shared energy variables.

6.4.6 Continuum and Soft Arms

Research direction. Generalise the screw‑theoretic kinematics to accommodate continuous deformations in soft and continuum arms, preserving the port‑Hamiltonian structure through infinite‑dimensional Dirac structures. The recent demonstration that distributed compliance and high‑performance control can coexist in a fully continuum arm challenges the assumption of incompatibility and opens the possibility of extending our framework to genuinely soft systems. This would require moving from finite‑dimensional Lie groups to infinite‑dimensional configuration spaces, potentially using Cosserat rod theory or reduced‑order strain modelling.

