Appendix B: Port‑Hamiltonian Dirac Structure Matrices

This appendix details the interconnection and dissipation matrices for the port‑Hamiltonian (PH) formulation of the 7‑DoF arm with series‑elastic actuators (SEA) at selected joints, as well as the Dirac structure that composes the arm with the hand at the wrist.

B.1 Augmented State and Hamiltonian

Recall from Chapter 3.3 that the augmented state for the arm is

x = \begin{bmatrix} q \\ p \\ \xi \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{7} \times \mathbb{R}^{7} \times \mathbb{R}^{m},

where q are joint angles, p = M(q)\dot{q} are generalised momenta, and \xi are elastic deflections of m series‑elastic elements (typically at joints 1,2,4,6,7). The Hamiltonian is

H(q,p,\xi) = \frac{1}{2} p^{\mathsf T} M^{-1}(q) p + V(q) + \frac{1}{2} \xi^{\mathsf T} K_s \xi,

with M(q) the mass matrix, V(q) the gravitational potential, and K_s = \diag(k_{s,1},\dots,k_{s,m}) the series‑elastic stiffnesses.

B.2 Interconnection and Dissipation Matrices

The PH dynamics are

\dot{x} = \left[J(x) - R(x)\right] \frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x) + g(x) u,

y = g(x)^{\mathsf T} \frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x).

For the arm, we use the canonical interconnection structure extended with the elastic states:

J(x) = \begin{bmatrix}
0_{7\times7} & I_{7\times7} & 0_{7\times m} \\
-I_{7\times7} & 0_{7\times7} & J_{q\xi} \\
0_{m\times7} & -J_{q\xi}^{\mathsf T} & 0_{m\times m}
\end{bmatrix},

where J_{q\xi} \in \mathbb{R}^{7\times m} encodes the coupling between momenta and elastic deflections. For series‑elastic actuators placed on joint j, the corresponding entry (j,k) is k_{s,k} times the gear ratio. In the simplest case (one SEA per actuated joint), J_{q\xi} = \diag(k_{s,1},\dots,k_{s,7}) but only for joints with SEA; others are zero.

The dissipation matrix is

R(x) = \begin{bmatrix}
0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & D(q,\dot{q}) & 0 \\
0 & 0 & R_{\xi}
\end{bmatrix} \ge 0,

where D is the joint damping matrix (positive definite), and R_{\xi} = \diag(r_{\xi,1},\dots,r_{\xi,m}) models energy loss in the elastic elements (e.g., internal friction).

B.3 Input Matrix g(x)

The control input u \in \mathbb{R}^{m} enters via the port that modulates the motor force applied to the series spring:

g(x) = \begin{bmatrix}
0_{7\times m} \\
P(q)^{\mathsf T} \\
I_{m\times m}
\end{bmatrix}.

Here P(q) is the coupling matrix from tendon displacements to joint angles (Chapter 3.4). For the fully actuated reference arm, P=I_{7\times7}; for underactuated designs, P has rank r<7.

B.4 Wrist Dirac Structure \mathcal{D}_w

The wrist is the interface between the arm (subscript a) and the hand (subscript h). Let the power ports be defined as:

· Arm output port: flow f_a (wrist velocity), effort e_a (wrench transmitted from arm to hand).
· Hand input port: flow f_h (hand base velocity), effort e_h (wrench applied to hand).

By Newton’s third law and kinematic compatibility:

\mathcal{D}_w = \left\{ (f_a, e_a, f_h, e_h) \;\middle|\; f_a + f_h = 0,\; e_a = e_h \right\}.

In matrix form, with port variables grouped as (f_a, e_a, f_h, e_h), the Dirac structure is given by

\begin{bmatrix} f_a \\ e_h \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & I \\ I & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} e_a \\ f_h \end{bmatrix},

which is skew‑symmetric and power‑continuous: e_a^{\mathsf T} f_a + e_h^{\mathsf T} f_h = 0. This guarantees passivity of the coupled system as proved in Theorem 3.3.

B.5 PH Simulator Initialisation

For numerical simulation, the matrices above must be discretised. A symplectic integrator (e.g., Runge‑Kutta 4 with energy preservation) is recommended. A Python skeleton is provided in Appendix D of the hand dissertation, adapted to 7 DoF.

