Appendix D: Comprehensive Spatial Grammar Ruleset

This appendix provides a complete, machine‑readable specification of the spatial grammar \mathcal{G}_{\text{arm}} introduced in Chapter 4.1. The grammar is defined by primitives, rules, and constraints. It can be used for motion generation, inverse kinematics, and as a high‑level programming language for prosthetic or collaborative arms.

D.1 Primitives

Each primitive is a parameterised motion. The parameters are continuous within joint limits.

Primitive Parameters Joint action Representation
Elevate(phi) \phi \in [\phi_{\min}, \phi_{\max}] \theta_1 = \phi Shoulder flexion
Abduct(phi) \phi \in [-90^\circ,90^\circ] \theta_2 = \phi Shoulder abduction
RotShoulder(phi) \phi \in [-180^\circ,180^\circ] \theta_3 = \phi Humeral rotation
FlexElbow(phi) \phi \in [0^\circ,150^\circ] \theta_4 = \phi Elbow flexion
Pronate(phi) \phi \in [-90^\circ,90^\circ] \theta_5 = \phi Forearm pronation
FlexWrist(phi) \phi \in [-60^\circ,60^\circ] \theta_6 = \phi Wrist flexion
DeviateWrist(phi, dir) \phi \in [-30^\circ,30^\circ], dir ∈ {radial, ulnar} \theta_7 = \pm \phi Wrist deviation
Reach(target) target ∈ SE(3) Composite (inverse kinematics) Macro
Contact(surface, point) surface (e.g., "table"), point ∈ \mathbb{R}^3 Event (reset map) Trigger
Release – Event End contact

The macro Reach(target) expands to an inverse kinematics solution that minimises a secondary cost (e.g., joint‑limit avoidance) while satisfying T(\theta) = \text{target}. The solution uses the null‑space projection as per Theorem 3.2.

D.2 Rules

Rules combine primitives (or composite expressions) into sequences, parallel motions, and loops.

Syntax Semantics Mathematical expansion
Seq(A, B) Execute A then B \theta(t) = \begin{cases} \theta_A(t) & t \in [0,T_A] \\ \theta_B(t-T_A) & t \in [T_A, T_A+T_B] \end{cases}
Par(A, B) Execute A and B simultaneously \dot{\theta}(t) = \dot{\theta}_A(t) + \dot{\theta}_B(t) (superposition)
Repeat(A, n) Repeat A n times \bigcirc_{i=1}^{n} A
Cond(cond, A, B) If cond true, A else B e.g., Cond(contact_detected, Stop, Continue)
Transport(A, mass) Execute A while compensating load mass Adds gravity compensation term to control law
Bilateral(L, R, delta) Coordinate left and right arms with phase offset delta \theta_R(t) = \text{mirror}( \theta_L(t + \delta) )

D.3 Constraints

Constraints are logical conditions that must hold for a valid derivation. They are checked at parse time or during execution.

Constraint Expression Remark
Joint limits \theta_i^{\min} \le \theta_i(t) \le \theta_i^{\max} For all i, t
Self‑collision avoidance \text{dist}(\text{segment}_i, \text{segment}_j) \ge \epsilon Typically 0.05 m
Singularity avoidance \det(J(\theta)J(\theta)^T) \ge \delta \delta = 0.01
Shoulder impingement \(\text{if } 70^\circ \le \theta_1 \le 120^\circ \text{ then }  \theta_2
Energy budget \int_0^T \tau(t)^T \dot{\theta}(t) dt \le E_{\max} Joules
Force closure (during contact) \rank(G) = 6 Grasp matrix from hand framework

D.4 Example Derivations

Example 1: Simple forward reach without load

```
Par(Elevate(30°), FlexElbow(40°))
Seq(Reach(T_cup), Contact(cup_surface, p))
```

Example 2: Overhead reach with gravity compensation

```
Seq(
    Par(Elevate(90°), Abduct(15°)),
    Transport(Reach(T_shelf), 0.5)   // compensate 0.5 kg
)
```

Example 3: Dual‑arm lift (bilateral coordination)

```
Par(
    Bilateral(L_arm, R_arm, 0.0),   // zero phase offset
    Par(FlexElbow(10°), Pronate(0°))
)
Transport(Seq(Reach(T_box), Contact(box_top, p)), 5.0)
```

D.5 Parsing and Execution

The grammar can be implemented as a context‑free grammar with production rules:

\begin{aligned}
\text{Motion} & ::= \text{Primitive} \mid \text{Seq(Motion, Motion)} \mid \text{Par(Motion, Motion)} \mid \text{Repeat(Motion, int)} \\
&\quad \mid \text{Cond(Expr, Motion, Motion)} \mid \text{Transport(Motion, float)} \mid \text{Bilateral(Motion, Motion, float)} \\
\text{Primitive} & ::= \text{Elevate(float)} \mid \text{Abduct(float)} \mid \dots \mid \text{Reach(SE3)} \mid \text{Contact(str, vec3)} \mid \text{Release}
\end{aligned}

A simple recursive‑descent parser can convert a grammar expression into a sequence of joint trajectories using the forward kinematics and, for Reach, an inverse kinematics solver (e.g., Levenberg‑Marquardt with null‑space projection). The source code is available in the accompanying digital repository.

